In the context of Internet of Things (IoT) auditing, an essential step of categorizing sensed data by IoT devices according to their security status is often overlooked, assuming secure storage from the start. Consequently, malicious data may be mistakenly classified as securely stored, compromising the integrity of devices. In our approach to resolving this issue, we employ two novel methodologies: 
1. The first methodology utilizes Formal Concept Analysis to ensure the robustness of training data against attacks; 
2. The second methodology leverages the training data for robustness by categorizing unknown data using both conceptual and operational views, supported by Dempster-Shafer theory and mass function calculation. This methodology also assesses the reliability of unknown data security status based on all device profiles. In our study, we showcased the efficacy of our categorization framework in an IoT auditing application, specifically an attack classification system for cloud-based IoT storage.

